   "en": "naval access level settings"
  },
  {
   "k": "179tm5i",
   "en": "Railway artillery cannot be assigned to any commanders directly, though like air wings, they can be assigned to land units and will follow their assigned units as best as they can. They can also be assigned to control groups, just like other land units."
  },
  {
   "k": "17mm61",
   "en": "Railway artillery doesn't attack other units directly, it instead applies a debuff to nearby enemy units engaged in combat, similar to ⟦0⟧. If a railway artillery unit has been recently moved, its bombardment bonus will decrease for 24 hours⟦1⟧ and take some time to recover. If multiple railway artillery are in range of a single battle, only the one with the greatest bombardment will take part.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "17xl3kb",
   "en": "shore bombardment"
  },
  {
   "k": "j559tv",
   "en": "Railway artillery are affected by ⟦0⟧, including both their movement speed (which decreases to up to ⟦1⟧ in low supply) and their bombardment. Unlike other land units, supply for railway artillery cannot be motorized at a unit- or army-level, so supply hubs must be motorized manually.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "157a5f9",
   "en": "Railway artillery can be damaged by enemy ⟦0⟧ and ⟦1⟧ air missions that target ⟦2⟧. ⟦3⟧ of damage from ⟦4⟧ missions spills over onto railway artillery in the state, while ⟦5⟧ of damage from ⟦6⟧ missions spills over onto railway artillery in the state. Damaged railway guns must be repaired with ⟦7⟧ and will appear in a country's ⟦8⟧ queue.",
   "frags": 9
  },
